%U A398738 1362,1384,1407,1431,1456,1482,1509,1537,1566,1596,1627,1659,1692,1726,1761,1797,1834,1872,1911,3577795,3577797
%N A398738 Lexicographically earliest sequence of positive integers such that no three of the points (n, a(n)) are collinear and no point (n, a(n)) lies inside or on the circle through any three earlier points.
%C A398738 Equivalently, a(1) = 1 and, for n >= 2, a(n) is the least k >= 1 such that the point (n, k) lies on no line through two of the points P(i) = (i, a(i)), i < n, and strictly outside the circle through any three of them. Without the circle condition the same rule gives A236335.
%C A398738 Let d2(n) = a(n) - 2*a(n-1) + a(n-2). For 3 <= n <= 15648069, d2(n) = 1 for all but 129 values of n, which form nine intervals. In this range a(n) > 4*a(n-1) only for n = 7, 13, 52, 2678, 106125, 147062 and 15648069, where a(n) = 41, 1132, 3577795, 373953245420, 53983796225378552, 839002211091037902981186 and 17485254894509800051106933625814581, respectively; for all other n in this range, a(n) <= 3*a(n-1). Each of the first six of these indices s starts a quadratic run a(n) = a(s) + (n-s)*(n-s+3)/2 (see the formulas) with 5, 39, 2626, 103447, 40916 and 15500914 terms; the first, fifth and sixth runs are followed by 1, 21 and 93 further terms before the next jump.
%C A398738 Let N = 4200886226749816982282591636107375. Then a(n) >= a(n-1) + 2 for 8 <= n <= N, so a(6) < a(5) is the only case of a(n) < a(m) with m < n <= N. See the Additional comments link for the proof, which uses the computed terms only up to n = 15648069.
%C A398738 Conjecture: d2(n) != 1 for infinitely many n, i.e., no run of consecutive n with d2(n) = 1 is infinite. The Additional comments link gives a sufficient condition for such a run to end; for n <= 15648069 it holds for every maximal run except the trivial run n = 3.
