%F A398738 a(n) = 40 + (n-6)*(n-5)/2 for 7 <= n <= 11.
%F A398738 a(n) = 1131 + (n-12)*(n-11)/2 for 13 <= n <= 51.
%F A398738 a(n) = 3577794 + (n-51)*(n-50)/2 for 52 <= n <= 2677.
%F A398738 a(n) = 373953245419 + (n-2677)*(n-2676)/2 for 2678 <= n <= 106124.
%F A398738 a(n) = 53983796225378551 + (n-106124)*(n-106123)/2 for 106125 <= n <= 147040.
%F A398738 a(n) = 839002211091037902981185 + (n-147061)*(n-147060)/2 for 147062 <= n <= 15647975.
%F A398738 a(n) = 2*a(n-1) - a(n-2) + n - 147061 for 147042 <= n <= 147060.
%F A398738 a(n) = 2*a(n-1) - a(n-2) + n - 15648068 for 15647977 <= n <= 15648067.
%e A398738 a(5) = 6: with P(1) = (1, 1), P(2) = (2, 1), P(3) = (3, 2) and P(4) = (4, 2), the points (5, 1), (5, 2), (5, 3) and (5, 4) lie on the lines P(1)P(2), P(3)P(4), P(1)P(3) and P(2)P(3), respectively, and (5, 5) lies on the circle through P(1), P(2) and P(4), which has center (3/2, 9/2) and radius 5/sqrt(2). The point (5, 6) is on none of the 6 lines and outside all 4 circles.
%e A398738 a(6) = 4: (6, 1), (6, 2) and (6, 3) lie on the lines P(1)P(2), P(3)P(4) and P(2)P(4), respectively, and (6, 4) is on none of the 10 lines and outside all 10 circles.
%e A398738 a(7) = 41: (7, 1), (7, 2), (7, 3) and (7, 4) lie on the lines P(1)P(2), P(3)P(4), P(1)P(4) and P(1)P(3), respectively. The triangle with vertices P(1) = (1, 1), P(3) = (3, 2) and P(6) = (6, 4) has area only 1/2, so the circle through them is large: it has center (-17/2, 45/2) and radius sqrt(1105/2), so (7, y) lies inside or on it if and only if (y - 45/2)^2 <= 1249/4, i.e., 4.829... <= y <= 40.170...; this excludes 5 <= y <= 40. The point (7, 41) is on no line through two of P(1), ..., P(6) and outside the circle through any three of them.
